  "slug": "open-electrophysiological-e8a5",
  "summary": "Population-aware and cross-cohort evaluations show that reported biomarker accuracy often drops when models meet unseen cohorts.",
  "synthesis": "Across these items, the dominant thread is whether biomarkers hold up beyond the cohort in which they were developed. The EEG Parkinson's study proposes a population-aware framework that enumerates 75 cross-cohort train/test configurations, and the resting-EEG TMS study reports accuracy falling from 81% internally, with failure to generalize stated in its title. Entropy-based measures recur as candidate markers: temporal vocal entropy improves depression detection over pooled acoustic baselines (AUC 0.646 vs 0.593), and temporal entropy flags unstable dynamics in public blood transcriptomic cohorts for psychiatric classification. The fMRI encoding preprint, which predicts component time series from LLM representations of story text, is methodologically distinct and only loosely related. What is new is the emphasis on explicit cross-population evaluation and on temporal entropy as a dynamical stability readout, rather than single-cohort accuracy.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-30"
}
